 	Here, \(Q_{\max}\) describes the finite adsorption capacity, \(K_{\mathrm{aff}}^{\ast}\) places affinity on a dimensionless standard-state scale, \(E_{\mathrm{ads}}\) expresses this affinity as an adsorption-energy descriptor, and \(\sigma_H\) quantifies intrinsic surface heterogeneity. This fingerprint allows adsorption data obtained from different isotherm models to be compared in a physically meaningful way, provided that the standard-state concentration \(C^0\), experimental conditions, fitted parameters, and uncertainty metrics are explicitly reported.
 	
